Mastering American English vocabulary requires shifting from passive memorization to active usage.\par
\par
Core Learning Strategies\par
\par
Learn Words in Context (Collocations): Never memorize isolated words. Learn words alongside the preposition or phrase they naturally pair with (e.g., instead of just learning "reluctant", learn "reluctant to agree" or "he hesitated, reluctant to accept").\par
\par
Use Spaced Repetition (SRS): Use tools like Anki or Quizlet to test yourself right before your brain is about to forget a word. This builds long-term neural recall far faster than traditional flashcards.\par
\par
Practice Active Recall: As soon as you learn a new word, immediately write 3 original sentences using it in different contexts, or speak them out loud.\par
\par
Understand Word Roots, Prefixes & Suffixes: Learn Latin and Greek roots (e.g., bene- means good, mal- means bad, spect- means look). Recognizing standard roots allows you to deduce the meaning of hundreds of unfamiliar words automatically.\par
\par
A Daily 4-Step Routine\par
\par
Consume Native Media: Read American articles (like The Atlantic, NPR, or Wired) or listen to podcasts for 15\endash 20 minutes daily.\par
\par
Capture 3\endash 5 Words: Highlight words you don't recognize. Avoid capturing more than 5 words per day to prevent overload.\par
\par
Look Up Contextual Meanings: Use Merriam-Webster (the standard American dictionary) to review real example sentences, synonyms, and correct American pronunciation.\par
\par
Apply Immediately: Force yourself to use those 3\endash 5 new words during conversations or daily journaling that same evening.\par
\par
